export const OXIMY_PRODUCT_DOCUMENT =
	"# Oximy product context\n\nThis document gives sellers a stable starting point.\n\nConfirm customer-specific claims before using them in outreach.\n\n## Visibility\n\n### Product\n\nVisibility shows how employees and agents use AI across an organization.\n\nIt attributes tools, models, accounts, agents, activity, and spend.\n\nIt combines network observation, first-party integrations, and optional endpoint depth.\n\nProduct page: https://www.oximy.com/visibility\n\n### ICP\n\n- Organizations with broad or growing AI adoption.\n- Security, IT, and finance teams that lack a reliable AI inventory.\n- Leaders that need adoption, risk, and cost evidence before expanding AI programs.\n\n### Buyer personas\n\n- CIO and CTO.\n- CISO and security leadership.\n- IT operations and enterprise architecture leadership.\n- Finance and procurement leadership for AI spend control.\n\n### Problems\n\n- Teams cannot see every AI tool or account in use.\n- Leaders cannot connect AI activity to users, teams, models, or cost.\n- Security teams cannot govern unknown AI usage.\n- Finance teams cannot explain fragmented AI spend.\n\n### Objections\n\n- Existing SaaS management tools already show approved applications.\n- Endpoint deployment creates operational concern.\n- Employees can interpret monitoring as surveillance.\n- Leaders do not yet have a formal AI governance program.\n\n### Pitch guidance\n\n- Lead with the buyer's missing AI inventory and decision evidence.\n- Explain the available observation depths without overstating coverage.\n- Connect visibility to governance, adoption, or spend based on the buyer's role.\n- Avoid fear-based shadow AI language without specific evidence.\n\n## Relay\n\n### Product\n\nRelay routes AI requests across providers using cost, quality, and latency goals.\n\nTeams can use gateway, route-only, and observe or shadow deployment modes.\n\nProduct page: https://www.oximy.com/relay\n\n### ICP\n\n- Product and platform teams that use several models or providers.\n- AI applications with material inference cost, latency, or reliability constraints.\n- Engineering teams that want routing control without rebuilding model selection logic.\n\n### Buyer personas\n\n- CTO and VP Engineering.\n- AI platform and infrastructure leadership.\n- Product engineering leadership.\n- FinOps leadership for large inference workloads.\n\n### Problems\n\n- One model does not provide the best result for every request.\n- Model changes create application work and operational risk.\n- Teams trade cost, quality, and latency through static configuration.\n- Provider incidents or limits reduce application reliability.\n\n### Objections\n\n- The team already built basic routing.\n- A new gateway adds latency or reliability concern.\n- The workload is too small for routing gains.\n- Model quality is difficult to measure automatically.\n\n### Pitch guidance\n\n- Start with the current routing decision and its operational cost.\n- Quantify request volume, model mix, latency targets, and spend before claiming value.\n- Match the deployment mode to the team's control and migration needs.\n- Position Relay as routing infrastructure, not another model provider.\n\n## Sidekick\n\n### Product\n\nSidekick is a company-owned AI workbench for employees and agents.\n\nIt provides approved models, connectors, skills, keys, governance, budgets, and audit controls.\n\nProduct page: https://www.oximy.com/sidekick\n\n### ICP\n\n- Organizations that want one governed AI work surface.\n- Teams that use several assistants without shared company context or controls.\n- Leaders that want employees and agents to use approved models and data connections.\n\n### Buyer personas\n\n- CIO and CTO.\n- AI transformation and digital workplace leadership.\n- IT and security leadership.\n- Business operations leaders scaling repeatable AI workflows.\n\n### Problems\n\n- Employees switch between disconnected AI tools.\n- Company context, connectors, and workflows do not travel between assistants.\n- Teams lack shared controls for models, keys, budgets, and audit.\n- Useful prompts remain personal and inconsistent.\n\n### Objections\n\n- Employees already use ChatGPT or Claude.\n- Another work surface creates adoption concern.\n- Existing assistants already support connectors and skills.\n- Central controls can reduce individual flexibility.\n\n### Pitch guidance\n\n- Lead with the missing company-owned layer across models, data, and workflows.\n- Show how approved skills make repeatable work easier for employees.\n- Position governance as an enabler for wider adoption.\n- Compare the complete workflow with existing assistants, not chat quality alone.\n";
